{
  "id": "web-services-conclusion",
  "slug": "conclusion",
  "title": "Conclusion",
  "category": "Spring Framework",
  "sections": [
    {
      "id": "example-booking-a-flight-ticket",
      "heading": "Example: Booking a Flight Ticket",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Rest/Web Services/Web%20Services/image.png",
          "alt": "image.png",
          "originalPath": "Web%20Services/image.png"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "What appears to happen:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Check flight seat availability",
            "Select and book a seat",
            "Make a payment"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "What actually happens:"
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Multiple independent systems (airline databases, payment gateways) communicate behind the scenes."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "text": "❗ A single airline app does not hold all airline data or payment systems—it connects to others."
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Rest/Web Services/Web%20Services/image%201.png",
          "alt": "image.png",
          "originalPath": "Web%20Services/image%201.png"
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 47,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "definition",
      "heading": "Definition:",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "text": "Web services are software systems designed to support interoperable machine-to-machine interaction over a network."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "They allow applications built in different programming languages and platforms to communicate using standardized protocols."
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 29,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "example-cab-booking-application",
      "heading": "Example: Cab Booking Application",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Uses location-based services (e.g., Google Maps API) for pickup/drop selection",
            "Uses payment gateways for processing payments",
            "Interacts with external services—regardless of the tech stack used in each"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Rest/Web Services/Web%20Services/image%202.png",
          "alt": "image.png",
          "originalPath": "Web%20Services/image%202.png"
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 27,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "definition",
      "heading": "Definition:",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "text": "SOA (Service-Oriented Architecture) is a design pattern that allows building applications from loosely coupled, reusable components communicating over a network."
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 20,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "features-of-soa",
      "heading": "Features of SOA:",
      "level": 3,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Reusable: Services can be used in multiple applications.",
            "Loosely Coupled: Minimal dependency between services.",
            "Interoperable: Works across different tech stacks.",
            "Scalable and Manageable: Can grow and be maintained easily."
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "blockquote",
          "text": "Example: In the airline booking app, SOA allows interaction with different airline systems despite platform differences."
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 45,
      "subsections": []
    },
    {
      "id": "implementation-of-soa",
      "heading": "Implementation of SOA",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Two major types:"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Type",
            "Full Form",
            "Description"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "SOAP",
              "Simple Object Access Protocol",
              "XML-based protocol with strict rules"
            ],
            [
              "REST",
              "Representational State Transfer",
              "Lightweight, flexible architecture style"
            ]
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 25,
      "subsections": [
        {
          "id": "key-concepts",
          "heading": "Key Concepts",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "Protocol for exchanging structured XML data",
                "Requires strict specifications",
                "Governed by W3C standards"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 13,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "components-of-soap-web-services",
          "heading": "Components of SOAP Web Services",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Component",
                "Description"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "WSDL",
                  "Web Services Description Language – Describes available services"
                ],
                [
                  "UDDI",
                  "Universal Description, Discovery, and Integration – Registry to locate web services"
                ],
                [
                  "SOAP Message",
                  "XML-based message format for requests and responses"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 32,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "soap-communication-flow",
          "heading": "SOAP Communication Flow",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": true,
              "items": [
                "Provider publishes WSDL and registers with UDDI",
                "Consumer finds service through UDDI and retrieves WSDL",
                "Proxy is generated from WSDL to handle communication",
                "Proxy serializes/deserializes SOAP messages"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "image",
              "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Rest/Web Services/Web%20Services/image%203.png",
              "alt": "image.png",
              "originalPath": "Web%20Services/image%203.png"
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 27,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "wsdl-elements",
          "heading": "WSDL Elements:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "<types>: Describes XML data types used",
                "<operation>: Functionality exposed (e.g., getGreeting)",
                "<binding>: Transport protocol details (e.g., HTTP)",
                "<service>: Web service endpoint (URL)"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "image",
              "src": "/Users/luffy/Desktop/Computer Science/Infosys/Spring Rest/Web Services/Web%20Services/image%204.png",
              "alt": "image.png",
              "originalPath": "Web%20Services/image%204.png"
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 22,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "java-support-jax-ws",
          "heading": "Java Support: JAX-WS",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "java",
              "code": "@WebService\npublic class GreetingService {\n  @WebMethod\n  public String greet(@WebParam(name=\"name\") String name) {\n    return \"Hello \" + name;\n  }\n}",
              "runnable": false
            },
            {
              "type": "list",
              "ordered": false,
              "items": [
                "@WebService: Declares a class as a web service",
                "@WebMethod: Marks a method to expose as a service",
                "@WebParam: Names the parameter for the SOAP message"
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 25,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "soap-limitations",
          "heading": "SOAP Limitations",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Drawback",
                "Explanation"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Heavyweight",
                  "Uses XML; large message size"
                ],
                [
                  "Slow",
                  "Parsing XML takes time"
                ],
                [
                  "Tightly Coupled",
                  "Changes in WSDL require client updates"
                ],
                [
                  "Only POST",
                  "Limited HTTP method support"
                ],
                [
                  "Bandwidth Intensive",
                  "More resource consumption"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 32,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "definition",
          "heading": "Definition:",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "blockquote",
              "text": "REST is an architectural style for building lightweight, scalable, and flexible web services using standard web protocols like HTTP."
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 19,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "rest-characteristics",
          "heading": "REST Characteristics",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Feature",
                "Explanation"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Lightweight",
                  "Supports JSON, XML, text, etc."
                ],
                [
                  "Resource-Oriented",
                  "Uses URIs to access resources"
                ],
                [
                  "Transport Agnostic",
                  "Commonly uses HTTP"
                ],
                [
                  "Flexible",
                  "Stateless, cacheable, layered, etc."
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 24,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "rest-principles",
          "heading": "REST Principles",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Principle",
                "Meaning"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "Uniform Interface",
                  "Standardized communication using HTTP verbs"
                ],
                [
                  "Client-Server",
                  "Separation of responsibilities"
                ],
                [
                  "Stateless",
                  "No client context stored on the server"
                ],
                [
                  "Cacheable",
                  "Responses can be cached"
                ],
                [
                  "Layered System",
                  "Intermediaries like proxies/load balancers"
                ],
                [
                  "Code on Demand",
                  "Optional ability to send executable code (e.g., JS)"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 43,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "http-methods-in-rest",
          "heading": "HTTP Methods in REST",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "table",
              "headers": [
                "Method",
                "Action"
              ],
              "rows": [
                [
                  "GET",
                  "Retrieve a resource"
                ],
                [
                  "POST",
                  "Create a new resource"
                ],
                [
                  "PUT",
                  "Update a resource"
                ],
                [
                  "DELETE",
                  "Remove a resource"
                ]
              ]
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 19,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "rest-example",
          "heading": "REST Example",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Request:"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "",
              "code": "GET /customers/101\nAccept: application/json",
              "runnable": false
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Response Headers:"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "",
              "code": "Content-Type: application/json\nCache-Control: max-age=3600",
              "runnable": false
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Response Body:"
            },
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "json",
              "code": "{\n  \"id\": 101,\n  \"name\": \"Alice\"\n}",
              "runnable": false
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 5,
          "subsections": []
        },
        {
          "id": "hateoas-hypermedia-as-the-engine-of-application-state",
          "heading": "HATEOAS (Hypermedia As The Engine Of Application State)",
          "level": 3,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "code",
              "language": "json",
              "code": "{\n  \"id\": 101,\n  \"name\": \"Alice\",\n  \"links\": [\n    { \"rel\": \"orders\", \"href\": \"/customers/101/orders\" }\n  ]\n}",
              "runnable": false
            }
          ],
          "wordCount": 0,
          "subsections": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "soap-vs-rest",
      "heading": "SOAP vs REST",
      "level": 2,
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "table",
          "headers": [
            "Feature",
            "REST",
            "SOAP"
          ],
          "rows": [
            [
              "Style",
              "Architectural",
              "Protocol"
            ],
            [
              "Data Formats",
              "JSON, XML, text",
              "XML only"
            ],
            [
              "Bandwidth",
              "Low",
              "High"
            ],
            [
              "Description Language",
              "WADL",
              "WSDL"
            ],
            [
              "Use Cases",
              "Public APIs, lightweight apps",
              "Financial systems, telecom, enterprise apps"
            ],
            [
              "Coupling",
              "Loosely coupled",
              "Tightly coupled"
            ],
            [
              "HTTP Methods",
              "GET, POST, PUT, DELETE",
              "Typically POST"
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "ordered": false,
          "items": [
            "Web services enable interoperability across platforms.",
            "SOAP is rigid, standardized, but heavyweight.",
            "REST is flexible, lightweight, and widely used for modern web APIs.",
            "Both serve distinct use cases depending on system needs (e.g., SOAP for secure transactions, REST for public APIs)."
          ]
        }
      ],
      "wordCount": 85,
      "subsections": []
    }
